DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Many programs make use of the standard library functions err(3) and
     warn(3) to generate errors and warnings for the user.  lliibbxxoo wants to
     pass that information via the current output style, and provides compati‐
     ble functions to allow this.

     The _f_m_t argument is one compatible with printf(3) rather than xo_emit(3)
     to aid in simple conversion.  This means these functions make unstruc‐
     tured data.  To generate structured data, use the xo_emit_err(3) func‐
     tions.

     These functions display the program name, a colon, a formatted message
     based on the arguments, and then optionally a colon and an error message
     associated with either _e_r_r_n_o or the _c_o_d_e parameter.

               EXAMPLE:
                   if (open(filename, O_RDONLY) < 0)
                       xo_err(1, "cannot open file '%s'", filename);

